  "why": "The paper proposes ORFuzz, a framework explicitly designed to discover a specific type of safeguard failure in LLMs, termed 'over-refusal,' where benign prompts are erroneously rejected by safety guardrails. The design incorporates multiple components specifically tailored to find these failures: 1) The failure oracle, OR-JUDGE, is a fine-tuned model designed to identify over-refusal instances by assessing query toxicity and the model's refusal. This is coupled with a reward function that directly incentivizes the discovery of non-toxic queries that are refused. 2) The mutation strategy includes 'Sensitive Words Mutators,' which are designed to probe the over-conservatism of safeguards by inserting words like 'kill' or 'bomb' into benign contexts. 3) The exploration strategy, 'Safety Category-Aware Seed Selection,' uses feedback from over-refusal rates in different safety categories to guide the fuzzing process toward areas where safeguards are more likely to fail. Finally, the paper demonstrates a successful 'bypass' (i.e., discovery of safeguard failures) by generating a benchmark dataset of 1,786 over-refusal instances and showing high over-refusal rates across 14 different LLMs.",
  "evidence": "Page 6, Section IV.B, 'Adaptive Mutator Selection and Refinement', 'Sensitive Words Mutators. These mutators operate under the assumption that safety sensitive words may trigger over-refusal more easily (such as “kill”, “bomb”). They aim to introduce new sensitive words or replace the existing terms with more sensitive ones.'\n\nPage 5, Section IV.A, 'Category-Aware Seed Selection', 'Building on this observation, we propose a novel, safety category-aware MCTS-Explore algorithm that effectively selects seed samples. It is a variant of the MCTS-Explore strategy [23], [24] and aims to improve the exploration of the seed space by prioritizing categories that exhibit higher over-refusal rates.'\n\nPage 8, Section V, '3) Evaluation Process', 'The purpose of this part is to determine whether the test cases effectively trigger over-refusal of the target model. ... Finally, we calculate the reward r ∈ [0, 1] of each test case: r = (p_over - p_toxic + 1)/2 which reflects the degree of over-refusal behavior. The higher the reward, the more likely the test case is to trigger over-refusal behavior. The rewards are used to update the seed selection and mutator refinement process.'\n\nPage 10, Section V.C, 'Transferability Analysis and Benchmark Construction (RQ4)', 'Leveraging these findings, we curated a new benchmark dataset, termed ORFUZZSET, comprising 1,786 queries from ORFUZZ’s output that successfully triggered over-refusal in at least three of the five initial target LLMs. We then evaluated ORFUZZSET on an expanded set of 14 diverse LLMs... As shown in Fig. 4 (left), ORFUZZSET consistently achieves the highest average ORR (57.37%) across all 14 models...'"
